## 02:15 — Read the spec and dataset
- Spec: https://github.com/AIProveRH/aiprove/tree/main/challenges/eth-backtest
  (frozen 1,440 ETH/USDT 1-min bars, real Binance spot bars, 24h window
  ending 2026-11-13 12:17 UTC; metric = net PnL after costs, ties break on
  lower max drawdown, held-out tail punishes overfitting).
- bars.csv: 1,440 bars, close 2028.68 -> 1960.59 (-3.36% over the day),
  max 2029.12, min 1906.99 (minute 1062). Structure: choppy sideways
  first half (~2000-2020), steady drop 2016 -> 1907 (minutes 720-1062),
  then a +54 recovery to 1960. A strategy must survive the chop, catch
  the drop, AND flip at the bottom to beat a plain short.
- No fee/slippage numbers are published with the challenge, so the cost
  model is stated explicitly and the design target is LOW TURNOVER:
  position held during bar t decided from bars up to t-1, entered at the
  open of bar t, PnL = position * (close - open); cost per fill =
  |dpos| * open * (fee + slippage), default fee 0.10% (Binance-style spot
  taker) + slippage 0.02% of notional, fixed 1 ETH.

## 02:16 — Candidate round 1 (same bars, same cost model)
- Benchmarks: always short +62.25 (1 fill, MDD 56.98); always long -67.11.
- Plain Donchian breakout: n=150 -> +65.27 (3 fills); n=200 -> +64.22
  (2 fills); n=100 -> +5.81; shorter windows whipsaw to -345.
- Keltner breakout best +46.69 (n=10, k=3.0). RSI+MACD best -3.74.
  VWAP mean-reversion best +2.47. SMA trend + RSI pullback best -20.61.
  ATR trailing-stop best -151.22. Plain EMA crossovers (checked earlier
  on this sample) -150 to -337. Turnover is what kills everything here.
- Donchian with asymmetric entry/exit windows (entry 200, exit 80):
  +77.17 (3 fills, MDD 35.11) — first candidate above the +75.89 bar,
  but only just.

## 02:18 — Candidate round 2 (the flip that catches the bottom)
- Donchian entry + percentage-bounce regime flip: while short, flip long
  when price bounces X% off the trailing 60-bar low; symmetric for longs.
  X=1.5% -> +108.63 (2 fills, MDD 21.62). The bounce rule fires once,
  at the real V-reversal, because after flipping the symmetric condition
  is far away — no whipsaw.
- Variants that FAILED: bounce with ATR-scaled threshold (1-min ATR is
  ~$2-3, so any k fires constantly: -69 to -3059, hundreds of fills);
  EMA-cross flip (-117 to -424, 40-95 fills); RSI flip (-76 to -241).
- 2-bar confirmation of the bounce (both closes beyond the level):
  1.2% -> +118.62 (MDD 19.22), 1.4% -> +108.63, 1.5% -> +102.12,
  1.8% -> +96.78, all exactly 2 fills.
- Plateau check (confirm=2): entry window 190-300 x bounce window
  30-120 x bounce 1.2-1.8% -> +89 to +119, always 2 fills. The chosen
  (200, 60, 1.4%) is the MIDDLE of that plateau, not its edge (bounce
  below 1.1% whipsaws; 1.4% sits comfortably above the cliff).
- Symmetry check: on the time-mirrored (up-trending) copy of the same
  bars the strategy goes long first and flips short at the top, netting
  +101.59 under the same cost model. It does not assume this sample's
  downtrend repeats on a held-out tail.

## 02:20 — Final choice
- Donchian breakout entry (200-bar window) + 2-bar-confirmed 1.4%
  bounce flip off the trailing 60-bar extreme. 4 parameters, no
  bar-index hard-coding, no lookahead (verified: signal for bar t reads
  only bars up to t-1 — every window, threshold and confirmation close
  is indexed at i = t-1 or earlier).
- Fills: short @ minute 739, long @ minute 1081.
  Flat 739 bars, short 342 bars, long 359 bars.
- Split-half robustness: first half 0.00 (no entry — the chop is avoided
  entirely), second half +108.63. No half is fitted separately.

